{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and the last ruler of the County of Tripoli. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born in 1152 AD and succeeded his father in 1152 as the Count of Tripoli.\n\n**Early Life and Succession**\n\nRaymond III was only a year old when his father died in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent on his behalf until he came of age. Raymond III was married to Eschiva of Arsuf in 1160 and they had two children together. However the marriage was short-lived as Eschiva died in 1168.\n\n*highlighted section part 1* **The Crusader States and the County of Tripoli**\n\nThe County of Tripoli was a Crusader state located in the Levant. It was established by Raymond I of Tripoli in 1104 and was one of the major powers in the region. The County of Tripoli was a vassal of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played an important role in the defense of the region against Muslim forces.\n\n**Marriage and Children**\n\nRaymond III married Philippa of Haifa in 1168. Philippa was a member of the noble family of Haifa and was a distant relative of the royal family of Jerusalem. Raymond III and Philippa had several children together including Bohemond IV of Antioch and Raymond IV of Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 2* **Death and Legacy**\n\nRaymond III died in 1187. His death marked the end of the House of Tripoli and the County of Tripoli was absorbed into the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III's legacy was as a skilled military leader who played an important role in the defense of the Crusader states. He was also a member of the nobility and a distant relative of the royal family of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 3* **Genealogy**\n\nRaymond III was a member of the House of Tripoli and was descended from the noble family of Toulouse. His father Raymond II of Tri